        <p>Construction safety features are critical in toddler clothing to prevent hazards and ensure child well-being. We implement comprehensive safety measures throughout the construction process, following EN 14682 requirements and best practices for children's garment safety. Every aspect of construction is designed with child safety as the primary consideration.</p>
        
        <p>Reinforced seams at stress points prevent seam failure during active play and repeated washing. We use double stitching at shoulder seams, crotch seams, and armhole openings where stress is highest. This reinforcement extends garment life and prevents potential seam separation that could create loose threads or fabric pieces posing choking hazards. Reinforced seams also maintain garment integrity through the frequent washing required for toddler clothing.</p>
        
        <p>No small decorative parts policy eliminates choking hazards from buttons, beads, sequins, and other small embellishments that could detach. When decorative elements are required, we use embroidered designs, heat-transferred graphics, or large appliqués that cannot detach. This policy follows EN 14682 requirements for children's clothing and reduces risk of choking or ingestion hazards. Brands can still achieve visual appeal through safe decoration methods.</p>
        
        <p>Safe drawcord lengths prevent entanglement hazards in hooded garments and waistbands. EN 14682 specifies maximum free ends and positioning for drawcords in children's clothing. We implement these requirements through elasticized drawcords that retract, drawcord channels that prevent free ends, and eliminated drawcords where alternatives exist. Drawcord safety is particularly important for hooded jackets and sweatshirts where entanglement risks are highest.</p>
